---
import Reveal from '../components/Reveal.astro'
import Section from '../components/Section.astro'
import Box from '../components/Box.astro'
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro'
import FaqItem from '../components/FaqItem.astro'
---

<Section layout="faq" spacing="none" id="faq" class:list={['content-section']}>
  <Box size="narrow">
    <SectionHeading context="faq" eyebrow="05 / Good questions" class:list={['mb-10 text-center']}>
      Frequently asked <span class:list={['serif italic']}>questions</span>.
    </SectionHeading>

    <Reveal>
      <div data-reveal="">
        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">Why not just use a CSS animation?</Fragment>
          CSS handles the animation, while JavaScript manages clones, visibility, and changing
          measurements. This library provides that lifecycle. Call refresh() after updating content
          or loading fonts, and provide a pause control for continuous motion.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">Does it work without a framework?</Fragment>
          Yes. The browser global is a single IIFE you can drop into any plain HTML page, WordPress
          theme, or Shopify template. There is no build step, no peer dependency, and no{' '}
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>window</code> pollution beyond the
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>Ticker</code> namespace.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">How does it handle accessibility?</Fragment>
          Visual clones are marked
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>aria-hidden="true"</code> and removed from the
          tab order, so screen readers and keyboard users only see your original content. The
          library also pauses animation when the document is hidden and when the user's system
          reports a preference for reduced motion.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">
            Is the native <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>&lt;marquee&gt;</code> element
            deprecated?
          </Fragment>
          Yes — both WHATWG and the HTML living standard list
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>&lt;marquee&gt;</code> as obsolete. This
          library gives you the behavior people still want from a marquee, on top of a normal
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>&lt;div&gt;</code> you fully control.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">Can I use it with React, Vue, or Svelte?</Fragment>
          Yes. Pass a ref to a DOM element into the
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>ticker()</code> factory and call
          <code class:list={['font-mono text-text']}>destroy()</code> from your unmount handler.
          There is no framework adapter to install and no peer dependency to ship.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">Does it pause when the tab is hidden?</Fragment>
          Yes. The library listens to the document visibility API and to the IntersectionObserver
          for the ticker element itself, so the animation only runs when the user can actually see
          it. This keeps CPU and battery usage in check on long-running pages.
        </FaqItem>

        <FaqItem>
          <Fragment slot="question">How big is the bundle?</Fragment>
          Size depends on the entrypoint and your bundler’s tree shaking. The locally built v2.0.4
          browser bundle is approximately
          <strong>4.3 KiB gzipped</strong>, plus <strong>0.5 KiB</strong>
          for the required stylesheet. There are zero runtime dependencies.
        </FaqItem>
      </div>
    </Reveal>
  </Box>
</Section>
